+# 2. Work item A — eliminate misleading fixed-area estimates
+
+## Problem to solve
+
+Previous review found hard-coded area values still used by production-facing code, especially:
+
+- `frontend/src/app/intervention-studio/page.tsx` — `studyAreaM2 = 250000`;
+- `frontend/src/app/page.tsx` — cost and water estimates calculated using `250000`.
+
+Search beyond these two examples: remove or correctly scope every production-facing fixed area assumption, including related report/optimizer defaults. It is acceptable for a test fixture or an explicitly named illustrative example to contain `250000`; it is not acceptable for an unlabelled real-world estimate to pretend it represents the selected study area.
+
+## Required engineering work
+
+1. Identify the authoritative source of each study area's analysis-window geometry and metadata. Use a consistent backend/API contract where possible rather than duplicating area constants independently in multiple frontend pages.
+2. Define clearly which quantity is being calculated:
+   - analysis-window/study-area area;
+   - land/ground area, if separately known;
+   - building footprint area, if available;
+   - intervention coverage area;
+   - cost/water basis.
+3. Calculate geographic area using a suitable projected metric CRS or a geodesic calculation. Do not calculate square metres directly from longitude/latitude degrees. Document which polygon is measured and how clipped cells/partial boundary cells are handled.
+4. Calculate estimates using the selected area's actual metadata/geometry and explicit model assumptions. If a required value (e.g., building footprint, density, intervention coverage) is unavailable, expose the assumption in the UI/API/report or label the estimate as illustrative. Do not silently invent a precise footprint.
+5. Update the Intervention Studio and home/dashboard pages so switching the selected area updates every relevant estimate. Ensure state changes and API responses are coherent; do not leave stale figures from a previous area.
+6. Search for and deal with other production defaults in reports and the optimizer. A fallback may remain only when it is intentionally safe, explicitly labelled, and covered by tests; an unsupported area must not quietly fall back to Delhi or a fixed 25-hectare district.
+7. Preserve consistent units and naming. Ensure percentages are not mixed between fractions (`0.35`) and percentage points (`35`). Align model/API/report fields and add tests for this contract.
+
+## Required tests
+
+- A unit test verifies the area calculation for a known projected geometry.
+- A regression test verifies that at least two different study areas produce correctly different area-dependent values when their geometric areas differ.
+- A test verifies that absent area/footprint metadata is handled transparently rather than silently converted to `250000`.
+- Search-based or static checks ensure no hard-coded `250000` remains in unlabelled production estimate paths. Any legitimate remaining use must be documented in the acceptance report with its file and reason.
+- Tests verify percentage/fraction conversions for cost, water, and coverage exports.
+
+---
