+# 6. Work item E — resolve the 10 m spatial-grid question honestly and technically
+
+## Problem to solve
+
+Previous evidence showed effective cell sizes around 38.95 m × 37.29 m for a 40 × 40 grid, while project language has referenced 10 m resolution. The current geographic-coordinate/local-distance approximation does not make a grid a true 10 m metric grid merely because a projected CRS is written in metadata.
+
+## First inspect the requirement
+
+Search the project specification, prior master prompts, UI copy, API models and current configuration to establish whether **10 m is a true required cell size** or only a nominal target/label. Record the source of the requirement in `docs/verification/SPATIAL_RESOLUTION_DECISION.md`.
+
+- If any product/API/UI contract currently claims that actual cell size is 10 m, treat that as a requirement and implement a defensible metric grid, rather than preserving a misleading label.
+- If the authoritative requirement describes 10 m as aspirational or nominal only, correct all claims so effective resolution is explicit and truthful. Do not represent 10 m as achieved.
+- Do not silently change the requirement or claim the issue is resolved merely by adding a note to a report. State the chosen resolution contract and evidence.
+
+## Preferred implementation when true 10 m cells are required
+
+1. Maintain GeoJSON interoperability: source/export GeoJSON uses WGS 84 longitude/latitude coordinate order (longitude, latitude); do not place projected metre coordinates in standard GeoJSON without a clearly separate nonstandard contract.
+2. Transform source geometries into a suitable local projected CRS with metre units for each study area. Use a maintained projection library already in the project (e.g., pyproj, if present) and an appropriate CRS selection/configuration per area. Avoid hand-written degree-to-metre conversion as the grid geometry contract.
+3. Create grid boundaries in projected metres. A cell advertised as 10 m must have 10 m projected width and height within a defined numeric tolerance, except explicitly documented clipped partial cells on the study-area boundary. Keep cell IDs stable/deterministic for the same geometry, CRS and configuration.
+4. Intersect cells with the study-area analysis polygon; do not use bounding-box corners as if they were official administrative boundaries. Continue labelling configured bounding polygons as analysis windows rather than official city limits.
+5. Define `resolution_m` / effective cell dimensions and the selected analysis CRS in the API response. Do not infer spatial resolution from arbitrary row/column counts. If users can request rows/columns, document whether these override or conflict with `resolution_m`, and reject ambiguous combinations rather than silently ignoring one.
+6. Revise maximum grid dimensions/cell count only as needed, with a safe upper bound based on profiling. Current 10–100 limits may be incompatible with a real 10 m grid across some analysis windows. Do not merely raise limits without evaluating CPU/memory cost. Add an explicit maximum-cell budget and appropriate client errors for overly large requests.
+7. Check every dependent path: digital-twin grid, inspect-cell, thermal simulation, diurnal profile, rasterization, cell-area calculations, optimizer area basis, GeoJSON exports and UI labels.
+8. Ensure area calculations use projected/geodesic geometry—not raw longitude/latitude degree units. Correct CRS transformations using consistent x/y coordinate order and test round trips.
+9. Add geometry tests that calculate cell dimensions in projected metres, validate row/column/cell IDs, test boundary clipping, check all supported study areas, and catch CRS/axis-order mistakes.
+10. Add at least one endpoint test demonstrating that the reported effective cell size matches actual geometry rather than a constant or label.
+11. Profile representative grids at target resolution for all five study areas. Record grid-cell counts, execution duration, peak memory if available, and the chosen maximum budget. If full-resolution thermal simulation is too expensive, keep map grid and thermal model resolution separate and clearly document the contract; do not mislabel one as the other.
+
+## If true 10 m implementation is blocked
+
+Do not falsify status. Correct misleading claims, document effective dimensions/CRS, mark the 10 m acceptance item `BLOCKED` or `FAIL` depending on the cause, and provide the exact remaining engineering task. C2 cannot be called fully closed while a documented mandatory 10 m requirement remains unfulfilled.
+
+---
+
+# 7. Work item F — preserve truthful provenance of modeled heat
+
+OSM features provide mapped urban geometry/context. They are not ground-temperature observations and do not establish independently measured heat fields.
+
+1. Search all UI strings, tooltips, reports, validation endpoints and documentation for wording that implies independent satellite ground truth, measured temperatures, observed outcomes, or empirical accuracy.
+2. If an endpoint creates an observed-like series from simulation output plus random/seeded noise, label it **synthetic self-consistency / simulated comparison**, not satellite validation or empirical ground truth.
+3. Reserve `observed`, `measured`, `ground truth`, and `satellite validation` for data with actual provenance, acquisition dates, units, processing lineage and licenses.
+4. For model-generated values, return/display model provenance and assumptions where the system supports them. Ensure the frontend does not overstate what the API returned.
+5. Do not invent satellite rasters, weather observations or training labels. If independently measured temperature data is absent, state that fact and retain an honest modelling-only label.
+6. Add tests for output metadata and a targeted source review of the relevant strings/labels.
+
+---
+
+# 8. Spatial data and runtime acceptance checks
+
+Confirm that live/previously acquired OSM snapshots are still present and usable. Validate:
+
+- all configured areas have raw and processed artifacts or an explicit area-specific failure record;
+- source IDs/tags, timestamps, attribution/license, download status, counts and hashes are recorded;
+- processed geometries are valid and clipped to their declared configured analysis windows;
+- feature counts reconcile among manifest, raw files and processed files;
+- no mock response is counted as successful live acquisition;
+- the runtime grid uses the processed OSM data through an explicitly tested mapping between OSM properties and rasterizer categories;
+- inferred building heights/unknown feature attributes are documented as estimates/defaults, not measurements;
+- empty/failed download handling does not silently create fake features;
+- the source and export CRS contracts remain consistent.
+
+If network acquisition is repeated, do not overwrite the only known-good data snapshot blindly. Write to a run-specific staging location, validate it, and promote it only when valid; preserve the prior snapshot and manifest. Capture download failures and timestamps per area.
+
