+# ANTIGRAVITY MASTER EXECUTION PROMPT
+## UrbanCoolSim — C2 Remediation, Metric Grid Correctness, Area Consistency, and Release Evidence
+
+**Role:** Act as the senior software engineer, geospatial systems architect, backend/API engineer, frontend engineer, test engineer, and release engineer responsible for closing the remaining C2 acceptance criteria in UrbanCoolSim.
+
+**Primary objective:** Inspect the current repository and the attached audit files, implement the required fixes in the actual source code, run meaningful tests against the final source, and create a reviewable evidence package. Do not merely produce a plan or another report. Do not claim completion without passing evidence.
+
+---
+
+## 0. Inputs and authority
+
+The user will provide these reference files. Find and read them before editing:
+
+1. `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Independent_Review_2026-10-26.md` — the narrative audit and failure explanations.
+2. `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Independent_Acceptance_Matrix_2026-10-26.json` — the criterion-by-criterion machine-readable acceptance matrix.
+3. The current UrbanCoolSim repository/archive — the implementation to modify.
+
+If one or both audit files are not available in the workspace, stop and report exactly which file is missing; do not silently invent their contents. If the repository is a ZIP, extract it to a new working directory and preserve the original archive unchanged.
+
+**Source-of-truth rule:** Use the audit report and matrix to identify findings, but verify each finding against the current code before changing it. If the current code differs from the audit because it has already been fixed, prove that with a test and update the matrix with evidence rather than reintroducing the defect.
+
+**Do not blindly trust any prior “PASS” report.** Re-run or inspect the underlying evidence. Keep historical logs unchanged; create new, timestamped final logs.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Mandatory operating rules
+
+1. Start with repository inventory, `git status` if available, current branch/commit, and a file hash manifest. Record the baseline before editing.
+2. Never overwrite or delete the user's original project/archive. Work on a copy or a dedicated branch.
+3. Do not remove unrelated features, model artifacts, reports, datasets, or configuration.
+4. Inspect the existing architecture before changing interfaces. Prefer minimal, cohesive fixes over rewrites.
+5. For every issue, trace the whole path: input schema → validation → service/engine → output schema → frontend/API consumer → tests.
+6. Any frontend UI change must follow this workflow:
+   - create a visual reference/mockup before editing UI code;
+   - implement the smallest necessary change;
+   - run lint, type checks, and production build;
+   - capture browser screenshots where browser tooling is available;
+   - compare the actual UI against the reference and report discrepancies.
+   If browser capture is unavailable, say so explicitly.
+7. Do not use synthetic or mocked evidence to claim that live external data was downloaded. Label mock tests as mock tests.
+8. Do not present OSM features as official cadastral boundaries, surveyed building heights, or observed thermal measurements.
+9. Keep the UI's product direction: city context and interactive spatial heat grid, not visible bounding boxes as the primary visualization.
+10. Never print secrets, user records, passwords, tokens, or personal data into logs or reports. If a potentially sensitive database is encountered, inspect only metadata/counts needed for the audit and exclude it from shareable deliverables unless explicitly approved and sanitized.
+11. Do not install dependencies globally. Use the project's declared requirements in an isolated environment. If network access is blocked, report the exact blocker and continue with checks that can run offline.
+12. Do not declare C2 closed or begin C3 implementation until the acceptance gate at the end passes or the user explicitly waives named criteria.
+
+---
+
+## 2. Phase A — Baseline and evidence audit
+
+### A1. Establish baseline
+- Record current date/time, OS, Python and Node versions, package manager, git commit/branch/status if available.
+- Inventory backend, frontend, tests, spatial data, model artifacts, database files, `.env*` files, and generated evidence.
+- Find and read:
+  - `AUDIT_REPORT.md`
+  - `README.md`
+  - `LOCAL_DEV_ENVIRONMENT.md`
+  - existing C2 reports, test matrices, manifests, and prior verification logs.
+- Record current SHA-256 hashes of tracked source files and the submitted audit inputs.
+- Identify all test commands from `pyproject.toml`, `pytest.ini`, `requirements*.txt`, `package.json`, and CI workflows. Do not guess test commands if configuration specifies them.
+
+### A2. Reconcile prior verification evidence
+The previous submission included backend logs with different counts (43, 49, and 58 passing tests) and a newer report claiming 70 passed. Determine:
+- which logs are historical;
+- which exact source snapshot each log corresponds to, if possible;
+- whether the final JUnit XML matches the final raw test log;
+- whether the final test command ran against the final modified source;
+- whether any tests were skipped, xfailed, or excluded.
+Do not delete historical logs. Create one new authoritative final run and label all earlier logs as historical when their source revision cannot be proven.
+
+### A3. Reproduce baseline failures
+Before fixing, attempt the checks relevant to the audit:
+- backend test collection and full test suite;
+- frontend lint;
+- TypeScript check;
+- frontend production build;
+- API smoke tests for invalid study area, grid resolution, simulation, optimization, and export;
+- spatial validator;
+- package/sanitization check.
+Record baseline failures with exact command and exit code. If a check cannot run due to missing dependencies/network, distinguish environment blockage from code failure.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Phase B — Fix remaining area semantics and estimates
+
+Audit and fix at minimum:
+- `frontend/src/app/intervention-studio/page.tsx`
+- `frontend/src/app/page.tsx`
+- any backend area metadata, optimizer, reporting, and export consumers discovered by call-site search.
+
+### Requirements
+1. Remove misleading hardcoded `250000 m²` production assumptions from user-facing calculations unless the page explicitly labels the value as a fixed illustrative 25-hectare scenario.
+2. Use a single authoritative study-area metadata source for analysis-window geometry and area. Do not maintain a second hand-coded area table that drifts from backend configuration.
+3. Distinguish clearly between:
+   - analysis-window area;
+   - land area;
+   - building footprint area;
+   - modeled intervention area/coverage;
+   - valid grid-cell area;
+   - any excluded/no-data area.
+4. Calculate areas using a suitable projected CRS or a documented geodesic area method. Do not calculate square metres directly from degrees or use a single latitude-based approximation as authoritative area.
+5. If geometry is rectangular and derived from configured bounds, call it an **analysis window**, not an official city/district boundary.
+6. If selected-area geometry or metadata is unavailable, fail explicitly or display a clearly labelled estimate; never silently fall back to another city's area.
+7. Standardize units:
+   - use one canonical internal unit for areas, preferably square metres;
+   - convert to hectares/km² only for display;
+   - define whether coverage fields are ratios in `[0,1]` or percentages in `[0,100]`;
+   - use names such as `coverage_ratio` or `coverage_pct` to remove ambiguity;
+   - test conversion at API, optimizer, UI, and export boundaries.
+8. Add regression tests proving that selecting a different study area updates derived values and that displayed/exported values agree with backend metadata.
+
+### Acceptance tests
+- No unexplained hardcoded 250000-area multiplier remains in production calculation paths.
+- Every configured area has a consistent authoritative area value or a transparently computed analysis-window area.
+- Invalid/missing area metadata cannot silently produce a Delhi/default-area estimate.
+- Ratio/percentage conversion tests include representative values such as 0.35 → 35% and 35% → 0.35 ratio, with no double conversion.
+- Exported reports and GeoJSON carry consistent area units and study-area IDs.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Phase C — Enforce thermal API input contracts
+
+Audit at minimum:
+- `/api/v1/thermal/simulate`
+- `/api/v1/thermal/diurnal-profile`
+- request/response schemas and shared study-area types;
+- the digital-twin grid and inspect-cell routes for consistency.
+
+### Requirements
+1. Use the existing supported-study-area enum/type or a single shared validator at the API boundary. Do not duplicate lists of IDs across routers.
+2. Invalid study-area IDs must return HTTP 422 with a stable, safe error body.
+3. Validate grid dimensions and resolution consistently across grid and thermal endpoints.
+4. If `grid_mode="metric_10m"` is requested, its resolution must be exactly compatible with the mode; reject conflicting `resolution_m`/`grid_resolution_m` values rather than silently ignoring them or reporting a false label.
+5. Invalid requests must be rejected before simulation or optimization work starts.
+6. Keep error messages useful but avoid leaking stack traces, filesystem paths, secrets, or database contents.
+
+### Acceptance tests
+- Unsupported study-area ID returns HTTP 422 on both thermal endpoints.
+- Supported IDs pass schema validation.
+- Conflicting metric-grid mode/resolution inputs return HTTP 422.
+- Missing/invalid resolution does not silently fall back to a misleading 10 m label.
+- Grid dimensions, response dimensions, and reported spacing agree.
+- API contract tests cover the same validator used by the production routes.
+
+---
+
+## 5. Phase D — Implement a real projected metric grid, or remove the false claim
+
+This is a critical scientific and geospatial acceptance criterion.
+
+### Required decision
+Inspect current product requirements and code. The audit found that `grid_mode="metric_10m"` could set `is_metric_10m=true` even though the grid was still laid out in EPSG:4326 and geometry metadata said it was not reprojected. Do not preserve that contradiction.
+
+**Preferred implementation if 10 m is a real requirement:**
+1. Select a suitable projected CRS for each study area, appropriate for its location and the required analysis; document why it is suitable. Avoid assuming one UTM zone is suitable for all five areas without checking.
+2. Transform analysis-window geometry from WGS 84 longitude/latitude into the selected projected CRS using a well-defined transformation library such as pyproj, with axis order explicitly controlled.
+3. Build the grid in projected metres with cell width and height of 10 m, except documented edge cells clipped to the study window.
+4. Clip cell polygons to the analysis-window polygon. Decide and document whether partial edge cells are retained, excluded, or represented with their actual area.
+5. Rasterize/aggregate OSM features using the projected geometries, preserving stable cell IDs and source-feature provenance.
+6. Transform cell polygons back to WGS 84 only for GeoJSON/API mapping outputs; GeoJSON coordinates must remain longitude, latitude in WGS 84.
+7. Report both:
+   - `geometry_crs` for returned geometry;
+   - `analysis_crs` for projected metric calculations;
+   - `target_resolution_m`;
+   - actual cell width/height or clipped cell area;
+   - valid/partial cell counts;
+   - any reprojection/geometry warnings.
+8. Ensure no API or UI claims “true 10 m” if the output geometry is still an unprojected geographic grid.
+9. Add limits to prevent pathological cell counts and memory exhaustion for large windows. Reject unsupported resolutions/dimensions with HTTP 422 and an explanatory safe message.
+10. Add tests that measure the projected cell polygon dimensions/areas with pyproj/Shapely or equivalent. Tests must not merely assert metadata produced by the same approximation being tested.
+
+**Alternative only if a true 10 m grid is not currently required:**
+- Remove/rename `metric_10m` and `is_metric_10m` claims.
+- Return `is_metric_10m=false`.
+- Expose the honest effective spacing and CRS.
+- Document the product decision and keep C2 open if the product requirement still demands a true metric grid.
+
+### Acceptance tests
+- A central full cell measures 10 m × 10 m within a defined numerical tolerance in the projected analysis CRS.
+- Edge cells are explicitly handled and have correct geometry/area.
+- The grid geometry is actually created in the projected CRS before output conversion.
+- Round-trip coordinates remain in the correct place and GeoJSON is valid WGS 84.
+- Grid generation respects maximum cell-count/runtime limits.
+- All five study areas pass the projected-grid contract, or unsupported cases are explicitly rejected and documented.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Phase E — Make packaging safe and reproducible
+
+### Requirements
+1. Preserve the developer's original local database and project files in the working tree; do not delete them from the user's local environment.
+2. Exclude populated runtime SQLite databases, WAL/SHM files, `.env` secrets, credentials, API keys, caches, virtual environments, `node_modules`, and temporary build artifacts from the shareable ZIP.
+3. Provide a sanitized, purpose-built test database fixture only if required by tests. Document that it contains synthetic records.
+4. Maintain one canonical spatial-data toolkit path; remove duplicate copies only in the generated distribution after verifying equivalence. Do not delete the user's original data.
+5. The package-validation script must fail if:
+   - a forbidden database/secret file is included;
+   - required source files are missing;
+   - expected deliverables are missing;
+   - the archive contains duplicate canonical toolkits;
+   - a checksum does not match.
+6. Create both required deliverables:
+   - `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Verified_Project.zip`
+   - `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Verification_Handoff.zip`
+7. The handoff should include the final report, machine-readable acceptance matrix, source patch, final logs, JUnit XML, dependency inventory, spatial validator output, manifest, and checksums. Do not include sensitive runtime records.
+8. Re-open both ZIPs after creation and verify their contents, file counts, forbidden-path checks, and SHA-256 hashes. A manifest entry alone is not proof that a file exists.
+
+### Acceptance tests
+- Both required ZIPs exist and can be opened.
+- Package validator rejects a fixture containing a forbidden `.db`, `.env`, or missing deliverable.
+- Package validator passes for the final sanitized project and handoff.
+- Manifest hashes match the exact files in the generated artifacts.
+- Source package has no accidental duplicate toolkit.
+
+---
+
+## 7. Phase F — Handle empty/invalid optimization results truthfully
+
+The previous smoke log showed an optimization request returning HTTP 200 with zero Pareto solutions, null recommendation metrics, and `physics_validated=true`.
+
+Trace the optimizer end to end:
+- request validation;
+- constraints and feasibility;
+- optimizer execution;
+- empty/non-converged results;
+- response schema;
+- frontend display;
+- report/export consumers.
+
+### Requirements
+1. An empty result must not be described as a successful recommended solution.
+2. Do not set `physics_validated=true` unless the defined physics-validation checks actually ran and passed on the returned candidate(s).
+3. If no feasible solutions exist, return a stable, documented no-feasible-solution state. Choose a status code consistent with the API's existing conventions (e.g. 422 for invalid constraints, or a documented 200 response with `status="no_feasible_solutions"` when the request was valid but the optimizer found none). Be consistent.
+4. Never fabricate recommendation metrics, Pareto points, costs, or cooling values.
+5. Add tests for empty Pareto sets, all-candidates-infeasible, missing objective metrics, and frontend/report handling.
+
+### Acceptance tests
+- Empty Pareto set does not claim a valid recommendation or physics validation.
+- API response distinguishes request validity, optimization completion, candidate feasibility, and physics validation.
+- UI and exports show “no feasible solutions” (or equivalent) rather than a successful recommendation.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Phase G — Complete verification against the final source
+
+Run checks in a clean, isolated environment where possible. Follow project-documented commands and lockfiles.
+
+### Backend
+- Verify declared dependencies and lockfile consistency.
+- Run test collection separately and capture its exit code.
+- Run the full backend suite and capture raw output, exit code, and JUnit XML.
+- Confirm the JUnit test count and pass/fail/skip totals agree with the raw log.
+- Run API smoke tests for all five study areas, invalid IDs, conflicting resolution settings, thermal simulation, grid generation, inspect-cell, export, and optimization edge cases.
+- Run the spatial validator.
+- Run Python compilation/type/lint checks if configured.
+
+### Frontend
+- Install from the repository's lockfile in an isolated project environment.
+- Run the declared lint command, TypeScript check, and production build.
+- Resolve lint/type/build errors introduced by this patch. Triage existing unrelated issues separately and document them.
+- If UI was changed, capture browser screenshots and compare them to the pre-change reference image. If browser tooling is unavailable, explicitly report the limitation.
+
+### Final-source integrity
+- Run the full suite only after all code changes are complete.
+- Record the source revision or a deterministic SHA-256 manifest of the final source.
+- Generate a new authoritative log set; label older logs historical.
+- Ensure every result in the final report refers to this exact source snapshot.
+- Do not edit source after the final run. If any source changes, rerun affected checks and regenerate the manifest.
+
+### Test evidence rules
+- Do not count offline mocked external-service tests as proof of live service availability.
+- Do not count a test as passing if it was skipped, xfailed, or not collected.
+- Distinguish:
+  - PASS — executed and passed;
+  - FAIL — executed and failed;
+  - BLOCKED — could not execute due to an external/environment limitation;
+  - NOT RUN — not attempted;
+  - WAIVED — explicitly waived by the user, with rationale.
+- Never convert BLOCKED or NOT RUN into PASS.
+
