+## 3. Confirmed successes
+
+### 3.1 Real spatial data and engine integration
+
+The manifest records 13,880 physical features across five study areas: Mumbai 941, Delhi 1,555, Singapore 2,166, Phoenix 2,885, and Tokyo 6,333. All ten raw/processed files independently pass the validator. The engine loader prefers the processed OSM snapshots and normalizes classes such as building, transport, green space, water, and land-use context into the rasterizer's internal properties. This closes the earlier issue where acquired files were present but the digital twin used only the tiny curated feature sets.
+
+The source still applies assumptions where OSM attributes are absent (for example, default building height). These must remain marked as inferred defaults rather than surveyed heights. The manifest correctly says official boundaries have not been verified.
+
+### 3.2 API study-area typing
+
+The `StudyAreaId` Literal is used at the thermal endpoint boundary and in the simulation request schema. This is the right pattern for getting FastAPI/Pydantic to reject unsupported strings with 422 before the computation path executes. The submitted regression tests cover both query-parameter and body-supplied invalid IDs.
+
+### 3.3 Export contract and percentage units
+
+The export utility now omits the invalid legacy `crs` member and describes WGS 84 coordinates as longitude/latitude. Default intervention coverage values are consistently expressed in a 0–100 range and include a `coverage_unit` marker. I independently ran `scripts/test_geojson_export.py`, which passed for all five areas.
+
+The output must continue to be described as an **illustrative blueprint**. Passing the coordinate/bounds tests does not mean the exported polygons/lines are surveyed proposed works, nor that they represent a computed cell-by-cell engineering layout.
+
+### 3.4 Reported test evidence
+
+The authoritative log ends with `70 passed, 11 warnings in 10.62s`; the JUnit XML records 70 tests, zero failures and zero skips. The matrix lists 70 PASS cases. The earlier 43/49/58 counts are plausibly successive development snapshots and are reconciled in the summary. This is credible submitted evidence, but it is not an independent execution from this audit runtime.
