+## 4. Blocker 1 — the claimed true 10 m projected grid is not implemented
+
+### Evidence in source
+
+In `backend/app/spatial/geo_digital_twin.py`, `generate_microgrid()`:
+
+1. Calculates `bbox_width_m` and `bbox_height_m` with `111320.0` and `cos(latitude)`.
+2. Uses those approximated widths to choose rows/columns.
+3. Builds `lons`, `lats`, and `grid_points` in longitude/latitude.
+4. Rasterizes source geometries that are still in WGS 84 coordinates.
+5. Returns `grid_coordinate_crs: "EPSG:4326"` and a `distance_method` explicitly stating the geometry was not reprojected.
+6. Sets `is_metric_10m` true based on the request flag, rather than a verified projected geometry contract.
+
+The OSM manifest independently says `configured_projected_crs_is_metadata_only_until_explicit_reprojection: true`. `docs/verification/SPATIAL_RESOLUTION_DECISION.md` nevertheless labels the equirectangular cosine-scaled approach as “projected” and the result as a true metric grid. These statements conflict.
+
+My direct engine call returned, for example, Delhi 129×131 cells, approximately 9.99×10.01 m according to the local approximation, **with the grid coordinate CRS still EPSG:4326**. This proves the values are approximate distances reported by the same algorithm; it does not prove the grid was constructed in projected metres.
+
+### Why its test is insufficient
+
+`test_true_10m_metric_grid_generation_and_contract` checks that the reported effective values lie between 8.5 and 11.5 m. Those values are computed from the same formula that uses `round(width_m / target_res)`. The test does not transform grid cell geometry into the configured projected CRS, measure the actual projected edges, test axis order/round trips, or validate projected boundary clipping. It can pass while the implementation violates the documented architecture requirement.
+
+### Required fix
+
+- Use the configured local metric CRS (or an explicitly documented equivalent suitable projection) with a real CRS transformation library, such as `pyproj`, and declare the dependency for the backend runtime.
+- Transform source geometries into projected coordinates for rasterization and create the grid in metres. Keep GeoJSON interchange in standard WGS 84 longitude/latitude; do not expose projected metre coordinates as standard GeoJSON.
+- Define exact cell geometry, stable cell indices/IDs, edge-cell policy, and the meaning of `effective_resolution_x_m` / `effective_resolution_y_m`.
+- Add geometry-based tests that transform actual grid-cell corners into the configured projected CRS and check the widths/heights against 10 m within a stated tolerance for all five areas. Include CRS round-trip/axis-order tests and boundary/partial-cell tests.
+- If that engineering work cannot be completed, change the status to `BLOCKED`, remove the “true metric 10 m” claims, and retain only the honestly labelled approximate mode. Do not mark AC-05 PASS.
+
+## 5. Blocker 2 — area values still do not share one authoritative definition
+
+### 5.1 Landing-page area table disagrees with current backend windows
+
+`frontend/src/app/page.tsx` contains:
+
+```ts
+const ARCHETYPE_AREAS_M2 = {
+  delhi_cp: 2106000,
+  mumbai_bkc: 2791000,
+  singapore_marina: 2768000,
+  phoenix_downtown: 2310000,
+  tokyo_shinjuku: 2249000,
+};
+```
+
+These values are used directly for CapEx, water, and HVAC estimate scaling. They do not match the current canonical backend bounding boxes in `backend/app/spatial/geo_digital_twin.py` / the export test. Applying the same local equirectangular approximation used by the implementation to those current windows gives approximately:
+
+| Study area | Landing-page value (m²) | Approx. current configured window (m²) | Landing value difference |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|
+| Delhi | 2,106,000 | 1,690,673 | +24.6% |
+| Mumbai | 2,791,000 | 2,291,408 | +21.8% |
+| Singapore | 2,768,000 | 2,324,182 | +19.1% |
+| Phoenix | 2,310,000 | 1,852,890 | +24.7% |
+| Tokyo | 2,249,000 | 1,831,767 | +22.8% |
+
+These comparison figures are **approximate and not a survey-grade area calculation**; they are sufficient to show that the values are internally inconsistent. The submitted API smoke log itself reports Mumbai `district_area_m2: 2291407.5`, while the landing page estimates against 2,791,000 m² for the same area.
+
+### 5.2 The primary area method still violates the prompt
+
+The intervention and report pages calculate area with cell counts and reported cell spacing, but their fallbacks use `111320 * cos(latitude)` against longitude/latitude bounding-box differences. The optimizer has the same degree-to-metre pattern. This is not the projected/geodesic area calculation required by the master prompt.
+
+There is also a semantics issue: the geometry used is a configured rectangular **analysis window**, not a verified administrative or district boundary. Describing its area as a surveyed “district footprint” overstates what is known. It should be called “analysis-window area” unless a real, provenance-backed district polygon is introduced.
+
+### Required fix
+
+- Create one backend source of truth for each area's analysis geometry and metric area. Use an appropriate projected CRS or geodesic area calculation.
+- Return the resulting area and its method/geometry provenance in study-area/grid metadata; the home page, intervention studio, optimizer, reports and exports should consume that shared value instead of duplicating numbers.
+- Separate `analysis_window_area_m2`, `building_footprint_m2`, `eligible_roof_area_m2`, and intervention coverage. Do not infer building footprint by treating an arbitrary average density as measured truth; mark any density prior as assumed.
+- Replace “district footprint” with “analysis-window area” where only a configured bounding rectangle exists.
+- Add tests that verify all consumers use consistent area for every area ID, area calculations use metric/geodesic geometry, and absent metadata does not silently switch to a stale hardcoded value.
+
+## 6. Blocker 3 — `resolution_m` parameters can be ignored or mislabelled
+
+The endpoint `GET /api/v1/digital-twin/grid` accepts `resolution_m` from 1 to 100 and exposes `grid_mode` alongside optional `rows` and `cols`. But current branching only selects resolution-derived sizing when `grid_mode == "metric_10m"` or `resolution_m == 10.0`.
+
+Consequences:
+
+- A request with `resolution_m=20` and standard mode does not create a 20 m grid; it can use the standard 50×50 dimensions while the requested value is not reflected in the effective contract.
+- A request with `grid_mode="metric_10m"` and `resolution_m=20` uses 20 m to choose dimensions but still sets the 10 m flag/note because `grid_mode == "metric_10m"` is true.
+- Supplying both explicit `rows`/`cols` and `grid_mode="metric_10m"` can result in the dimension values being ignored. The API does not reject the ambiguous combination.
+- `PhysicsSimulationRequest` defines `grid_resolution_m`, but `backend/app/api/thermal_router.py` does not use that field to configure the grid. Thermal simulation calls the standard grid generator with `rows` and `cols`; it does not use the metric-10 m mode. Thus the digital-twin metric flag is not a consistent full-stack grid contract.
+
+### Required fix
+
+Choose one clear API contract:
+
+1. Either support arbitrary `resolution_m` values by creating that metric grid, with a suitable cell budget and correct projected geometry; or
+2. Limit the parameter to supported options and reject unsupported values with HTTP 422.
+
+Reject or define precedence for conflicting `grid_mode`, `resolution_m`, `rows`, and `cols`; never silently ignore one. Use a shared grid-definition object/contract across digital twin, cell inspection, thermal simulation and diurnal-profile where applicable. Add tests for 10 m, 20 m, conflicting dimension/resolution parameters, invalid values, maximum-cell errors and consistent metadata across endpoints.
+
+## 7. Blocker 4 — the packaging/evidence gate is not fully enforceable
+
+### What passed
+
+The uploaded outer ZIP is clean of database files, raw `.env` files and trailing-dot toolkit paths. This part is a genuine success.
+
+### What remains incomplete
+
+- `artifacts/c2-final-closure/SHA256_MANIFEST.txt` lists `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Verified_Project.zip` and `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Verification_Handoff.zip`, but neither file is present inside the submitted archive. I verified the seven available manifest entries; both missing ZIP entries cannot be verified.
+- `scripts/test_packaging_safety.py` prints an informational message and succeeds when there are no `artifacts/c2-final-closure/*.zip` files. Therefore it can report `OVERALL PACKAGING SAFETY: PASS` without inspecting the required final release ZIP.
+- The packing script and forbidden-pattern tests enumerate several specific `.env` forms but do not consistently reject the broader `.env.*` family (except `.env.example`) required by the written safety policy. The checks should include environment variants and common database backups/sidecars, and should inspect the actual generated output.
+- The current uploaded bundle does not contain the claimed final project ZIP or handoff ZIP as subfiles. They may have been generated locally, but this submitted archive is not sufficient evidence of them.
+
+### Required fix
+
+- Make packaging verification fail if any required output archive is missing.
+- Build the archives first; then scan the exact ZIP contents (not only the working tree) for databases/sidecars, `.env.*` except `.env.example`, credentials/private-key patterns, generated caches, and duplicate toolkit paths.
+- Create `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Verified_Project.zip` and `UrbanCoolSim_C2_Verification_Handoff.zip`, hash both, verify every manifest entry, and deliver those files directly alongside the reviewable source archive.
+- Make the acceptance matrix indicate `BLOCKED` for archive verification if the relevant ZIP is missing—not `PASS`.
+
+## 8. Additional issue to carry into the C3 design gate
+
+The submitted `api_smoke_check.log` records an optimization HTTP 200 response for Mumbai with `pareto_solutions_count: 0`; its `recommended_solution` contains `mean_temp_reduction_c: null` and `total_capex_usd: null`, while `physics_validated: true`.
+
+That is not a valid successful optimization result, even if the endpoint itself returned HTTP 200. The response must either return a nonempty, valid Pareto/recommendation result or explicitly state a no-feasible-solution/error status without claiming physics validation. This should be fixed or be an explicit C3 entry-gate test before introducing persistence, background workers, scenario selection, and reports that depend on optimization results.
+
+## 9. Verification caveats and record quality
+
+- The submitted JUnit XML and authoritative pytest log agree on **70 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped**. The independent audit environment could not repeat the full suite because `bcrypt` was absent and network installation was unavailable. The source requirements do declare the missing dependency, so this is an audit-environment limitation rather than a proven project defect.
+- Independent spatial validation and the GeoJSON export test passed.
+- The frontend closure build log explicitly says Next.js skipped its built-in type and lint validation, but separate TypeScript and lint commands are listed in the environment ledger; ESLint reports hook-dependency warnings, not errors. Those checks are reported, not independently rerun in this environment.
+- The report should avoid claiming the exact nested project/handoff artifacts are verified until those files are present and their hashes are checked.
+- OSM is a downloaded, timestamped community-mapped vector snapshot. It is not an official boundary dataset and does not constitute observed heat measurements. The app correctly labels thermal values as modelled; avoid describing the packaged snapshot as a live network feed.
+
+## 10. Final closure checklist
+
+C2 may be reconsidered only after all applicable items below are evidenced:
+
+- [ ] Replace the equirectangular grid approximation with a real projected metric grid, or formally change the requirement and remove all false true-10-m claims.
+- [ ] Add geometry-based projected cell-size tests for all five study areas.
+- [ ] Unify backend/frontend/optimizer area derivation and fix the approximately 19–25% home-page discrepancies.
+- [ ] Label configured rectangles as analysis windows, not official district boundaries.
+- [ ] Make `resolution_m`, `grid_mode`, `rows`, `cols`, inspection and thermal simulation contracts consistent; reject ambiguous requests.
+- [ ] Ensure over-budget grid requests produce intentional client errors at all relevant API routes.
+- [ ] Make the packaging test fail if a required archive is missing and harden secret/database-backup patterns.
+- [ ] Include and verify the exact project ZIP and handoff ZIP; regenerate the SHA-256 manifest after packaging.
+- [ ] Fix or explicitly gate the empty optimization response that marks a null-valued recommendation as `physics_validated`.
+- [ ] Run the final backend suite, spatial validator, export tests, frontend lint, TypeScript check, frontend production build and API smoke tests on the same final source snapshot.
+- [ ] Regenerate the test matrix and report each criterion as `PASS`, `FAIL`, `BLOCKED`, or `NOT RUN` based on actual evidence.
+
+**Final decision: C2 is not yet closed. Do not treat the current `PASS / CLOSED` report as authoritative until the mandatory spatial-grid and area-contract defects above are resolved.**
